<body data-lang="both">

<header class="topbar">
  <div class="brand">
    <span class="sun" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <div class="titles">
      <strong>Why Is the Sky Blue?</strong>
      <small>为什么天空是蓝色的？ · Bilingual side-by-side / 双语对照</small>
    </div>
  </div>
  <nav class="nav" aria-label="Sections">
    <a href="#sec-toddler" data-sec="toddler">Toddler · 幼儿</a>
    <a href="#sec-teen" data-sec="teen">Teen · 高中</a>
    <a href="#sec-phd" data-sec="phd">PhD · 博士</a>
  </nav>
  <div class="toggle" role="group" aria-label="Language display">
    <button data-lang="both" class="active" aria-pressed="true">双语 Both</button>
    <button data-lang="en" aria-pressed="false">EN</button>
    <button data-lang="zh" aria-pressed="false">中文</button>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="grid">

  <!-- ============ Section 1 : Toddler ============ -->
  <section class="panel en" lang="en" data-sec="toddler" id="sec-toddler">
    <span class="tag">English</span>
    <h2>For a 3-Year-Old Toddler</h2>
    <p>You know how sunlight looks white, like the light in your house? Well, that white light is actually a secret mix of <em>all</em> the rainbow colors — red, orange, yellow, green, blue, and purple — hiding together!</p>
    <p>When the sunlight zooms into the sky, it bumps into tiny, tiny bits of air, smaller than anything you could ever see. Most of the colors just go straight through, like running past you on the playground. But the blue color is like a hyper little kid — it can't stop bouncing around! It splashes everywhere, bouncing off all those tiny air bits and scattering all over the sky.</p>
    <p>So when you look up, all that bouncy blue light is coming at your eyes from every direction. That's why the sky is blue! And at sunset, the sunlight has to travel through <em>way</em> more air to reach you, so all the blue gets bounced away before it gets there, and the sky turns red and orange instead.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel zh" lang="zh-CN" data-sec="toddler">
    <span class="tag">中文</span>
    <h2>写给三岁的小朋友</h2>
    <p>你知道吗，阳光看起来是白色的，就像家里的灯光一样？其实呀，这种白色的光是所有彩虹颜色——红、橙、黄、绿、蓝、紫——偷偷混在一起藏起来的！</p>
    <p>当阳光“嗖”地射进天空时，它会撞上极小极小的空气微粒，比你见过的任何东西都小。大部分颜色直接穿过去，就像在操场上从你身边跑过去一样。但是蓝色就像一个精力超旺的小朋友——它停不下来，到处乱蹦！它到处飞溅，撞在那些小空气微粒上，把蓝色撒满整个天空。</p>
    <p>所以当你抬头看的时候，到处乱蹦的蓝光正从四面八方涌进你的眼睛里。这就是天空是蓝色的原因！到了日落的时候，阳光要穿过<em>多得多的</em>空气才能到达你，所以蓝色还没到就被全部弹走了，天空就变成红色和橙色啦。</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Section 2 : Teen ============ -->
  <section class="panel en" lang="en" data-sec="teen" id="sec-teen">
    <span class="tag">English</span>
    <h2>For a High School Teenager</h2>
    <p>Sunlight looks white, but it's actually a combination of all the visible wavelengths, from red (about 700 nm) to violet (about 400 nm). When sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere, it encounters molecules of nitrogen and oxygen that are much smaller than the wavelength of light.</p>
    <p>This triggers <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, where light waves interact with molecules and re-radiate in many directions. The key detail: the scattering intensity is inversely proportional to the <em>fourth power</em> of the wavelength:</p>
    <div class="math" data-math="I \propto \frac{1}{\lambda^4}"></div>
    <p>This means shorter wavelengths scatter enormously more than longer ones. Blue light (around 450 nm) scatters roughly 5–10× more strongly than red light (around 650 nm). So even though sunlight contains all colors, the blue component gets scattered in every direction throughout the atmosphere, and that scattered light is what fills your view when you look up.</p>
    <p><strong>Two common follow-ups:</strong></p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Why not violet?</strong> Violet scatters even more than blue, but sunlight contains far less violet than blue, and our eyes are much less sensitive to violet. The mix of all the scattered short wavelengths registers as blue.</li>
      <li><strong>Why is sunset orange/red?</strong> At sunset, sunlight travels through much more atmosphere (a longer slant path) before reaching your eyes. Most of the blue gets scattered out of that direct beam long before it arrives, leaving the redder wavelengths to dominate. The same scattering also paints the sky near the horizon orange during sunrise and sunset.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="panel zh" lang="zh-CN" data-sec="teen">
    <span class="tag">中文</span>
    <h2>写给高中生</h2>
    <p>阳光看起来是白色的，但它实际上是所有可见光波长的组合，从红色（约 700 nm）到紫色（约 400 nm）。当阳光进入地球大气层时，会遇到氮气和氧气的分子，这些分子比光的波长短得多。</p>
    <p>这会引发<strong>瑞利散射</strong>：光波与分子相互作用后，向各个方向重新辐射出去。关键细节是：散射强度与波长的<em>四次方</em>成反比：</p>
    <div class="math" data-math="I \propto \frac{1}{\lambda^4}"></div>
    <p>这意味着短波长的光比长波长的光散射得多得多。蓝光（约 450 nm）的散射强度大约是红光（约 650 nm）的 5–10 倍。所以尽管阳光包含所有颜色，蓝色成分会在整个大气层中朝各个方向被散射，你抬头看到的正是这些散射光铺满了视野。</p>
    <p><strong>两个常见的追问：</strong></p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>为什么不是紫色？</strong>紫色的散射比蓝色还多，但阳光中紫色远远少于蓝色，而且我们的眼睛对紫色也敏感得多。所有被散射的短波长混合在一起，看起来就是蓝色。</li>
      <li><strong>为什么日落是橙色/红色？</strong>日落时，阳光在你看到之前要穿过多得多的大气层（斜穿路径更长）。到达之前，大部分蓝色早就从直射光束中被散射掉了，只剩下偏红的波长占主导。同样的散射也让日出和日落时靠近地平线的天空染上橙色。</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Section 3 : PhD ============ -->
  <section class="panel en" lang="en" data-sec="phd" id="sec-phd">
    <span class="tag">English</span>
    <h2>For a Physics PhD</h2>
    <p>The color of the daytime sky is a consequence of elastic light–matter interactions at the scale of individual gas molecules — specifically, Rayleigh scattering. The relevant physics:</p>
    <p><strong>1. The dipole picture.</strong> An incident electric field <span class="m" data-math="E = E_0 e^{i(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{r} - \omega t)}"></span> polarizes a molecule, inducing a dipole <span class="m" data-math="\mathbf{p} = \alpha \mathbf{E}"></span>, where <span class="m" data-math="\alpha"></span> is the (polarizability) and for small particles is essentially frequency-independent over the visible range. The re-radiated field from this oscillating dipole is the source of the scattered light. The differential scattering cross section per molecule, for unpolarized incident light, is the classical Thomson/Rayleigh result:</p>
    <div class="math" data-math="\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega} = \frac{3}{32\pi}\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\right)^4 \alpha^2 (1 + \cos^2\theta)"></div>
    <p>hence the <span class="m" data-math="1/\lambda^4"></span> spectral dependence. Note the important distinction: this <span class="m" data-math="\lambda"></span>-dependence comes from the <em>far-field radiation pattern of the induced dipole</em> (dipole radiation scales as <span class="m" data-math="(\omega/\omega_0)^4"></span> per unit polarizability), not from resonance absorption — which is why Rayleigh scattering is "elastic" and why a single-polarizability model suffices when <span class="m" data-math="\lambda \gg"></span> molecular size.</p>
    <p><strong>2. Angular structure and polarization.</strong> The <span class="m" data-math="(1+\cos^2\theta)"></span> factor means scattering is equal in the forward and backward directions and zero at 90° <em>for the vertical component</em> — but summing over polarization yields net forward/backward enhancement. The polarization degree at scattering angle <span class="m" data-math="\theta"></span> is <span class="m" data-math="\frac{\sin^2\theta}{1+\cos^2\theta}"></span>, reaching 100% at 90°. This is directly observable: the sky is partially linearly polarized, and maximum polarization occurs roughly 90° from the solar direction (which is the basis of polarization filters and why bees exploit it for navigation).</p>
    <p><strong>3. Spectral shape and the blue-vs-violet puzzle.</strong> The Rayleigh spectrum <span class="m" data-math="I(\lambda) \propto \lambda^{-4}"></span> strongly favors the blue end of the visible band, but the perceived color is blue rather than violet for three compounding reasons:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The solar spectrum (close to a ~5800 K blackbody) has substantially lower flux at 400 nm than at 450 nm;</li>
      <li>Human photopic sensitivity (V(λ)) falls steeply toward 400 nm (cone sensitivity, especially S-cone contribution, is much lower there);</li>
      <li>The upper atmosphere (~15–30 km, the ozone-rich layer) absorbs much of the UV and some violet via the Chappuis and Hartley bands, and atmospheric <em>aerosols</em> (Mie scattering) add a wavelength-independent-ish component that slightly muddies the hue.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The net effect is a peak in perceived chromaticity near 480 nm — cyan-leaning blue.</p>
    <p><strong>4. Path-length integration and twilight colors.</strong> The observed radiance at the eye is an integral along the line of sight through the atmosphere: direct solar flux is attenuated by <span class="m" data-math="\exp[-\tau(\lambda)]"></span>, where the optical depth <span class="m" data-math="\tau(\lambda) \propto \lambda^{-4} \sec \zeta"></span> (plus aerosol and molecular absorption contributions), while in-scattered light is a sum over the path of source function terms involving the phase function and local optical depth. At solar zenith angle <span class="m" data-math="\zeta \to 90°"></span>, the direct beam's blue is extinguished (high <span class="m" data-math="\tau"></span> for blue, low <span class="m" data-math="\tau"></span> for red), leaving the reddish direct component and the scattered light in the sky — hence orange sunsets. The same formalism, extended, explains why the sky fades through blue → pale → lavender → dark at twilight, and why, on a very clean high-altitude day (e.g., above 4 km), the zenith can look a deeper, almost indigo blue — less path-length for in-scatter mixing and less low-wavelength extinction of the direct beam.</p>
    <p><strong>5. When the model breaks.</strong> Rayleigh scattering requires the particle size parameter <span class="m" data-math="x = 2\pi r/\lambda \ll 1"></span> (for N<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub>: <span class="m" data-math="r \sim 0.15"></span> nm, <span class="m" data-math="x \sim 10^{-3}"></span> at 500 nm — well within regime). Once aerosols, water droplets, or ice crystals approach <span class="m" data-math="x \sim 1"></span>, Mie scattering takes over: the angular dependence becomes strongly forward-peaked, the wavelength dependence flattens toward <span class="m" data-math="1/\lambda^0"></span>–<span class="m" data-math="1/\lambda^{0.1}"></span>, and you get white clouds, forward-scattered halos, and iridescence. The blue sky is thus strictly a property of <em>molecular</em> (not particulate) air — which is also why, on hazy days, the blue is muted and the sky whitens near the horizon.</p>
    <p><strong>A note on the "4" in <span class="m" data-math="\lambda^{-4}"></span>:</strong> it is sometimes over-mystified. It is simply the product of two scalings: the induced dipole moment scales as <span class="m" data-math="\alpha E"></span> (no frequency dependence for the polarizability of small molecules), and the dipole's radiated power scales as <span class="m" data-math="\omega^4"></span> (a far-field, dipole-field kinematic result). So the whole visible spectrum behavior follows from one number — the molecular polarizability — and basic EM.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel zh" lang="zh-CN" data-sec="phd">
    <span class="tag">中文</span>
    <h2>写给物理学博士生</h2>
    <p>白天的天空颜色是单个气体分子尺度上光–物质弹性相互作用的结果——具体来说，就是瑞利散射。相关的物理如下：</p>
    <p><strong>1. 偶极子图像。</strong>入射电场 <span class="m" data-math="E = E_0 e^{i(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{r} - \omega t)}"></span> 使分子极化，感应出偶极矩 <span class="m" data-math="\mathbf{p} = \alpha \mathbf{E}"></span>，其中 <span class="m" data-math="\alpha"></span> 是（极化率），对小粒子而言在可见光范围内基本与频率无关。这个振荡偶极子重新辐射出的电场就是散射光的来源。对于非偏振入射光，每个分子的微分散射截面由经典的汤普森/瑞利（Thomson/Rayleigh）结果给出：</p>
    <div class="math" data-math="\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega} = \frac{3}{32\pi}\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\right)^4 \alpha^2 (1 + \cos^2\theta)"></div>
    <p>由此得到 <span class="m" data-math="1/\lambda^4"></span> 的频谱依赖关系。注意一个重要的区别：这个 <span class="m" data-math="\lambda"></span> 依赖关系来自<em>感应偶极子的远场辐射图案</em>（单位极化率的偶极辐射按 <span class="m" data-math="(\omega/\omega_0)^4"></span> 缩放），而不是来自共振吸收——这正是瑞利散射是“弹性”的原因，也是当 <span class="m" data-math="\lambda \gg"></span> 分子尺寸时单一极化率模型就够用的原因。</p>
    <p><strong>2. 角分布与偏振。</strong><span class="m" data-math="(1+\cos^2\theta)"></span> 因子意味着散射在前向和后向相等，对<em>垂直分量</em>而言在 90° 处为零——但对所有偏振求和后，前向/后向呈现净增强。散射角 <span class="m" data-math="\theta"></span> 处的偏振度为 <span class="m" data-math="\frac{\sin^2\theta}{1+\cos^2\theta}"></span>，在 90° 处达到 100%。这可以直接观测到：天空是部分线偏振的，最大偏振出现在距太阳方向约 90° 的位置（这正是偏振滤光片的基础，也是蜜蜂利用它导航的原因）。</p>
    <p><strong>3. 频谱形状与“蓝 vs. 紫”之谜。</strong>瑞利谱 <span class="m" data-math="I(\lambda) \propto \lambda^{-4}"></span> 强烈偏向可见光带的蓝色端，但感知到的颜色是蓝色而非紫色，这是三个叠加原因造成的：</p>
    <ul>
      <li>太阳光谱（接近 ~5800 K 的黑体）在 400 nm 处的通量比 450 nm 处低得多；</li>
      <li>人眼的明视觉敏感度（V(λ)）向 400 nm 方向急剧下降（锥体细胞敏感度，尤其是 S 锥体的贡献，在那里低得多）；</li>
      <li>高层大气（约 15–30 km，富含臭氧的层）通过查皮耶带（Chappuis）和哈特利带（Hartley）吸收了大量紫外线和部分紫光，而大气中的<em>气溶胶</em>（米氏散射）又加入了一个近似与波长无关的成分，使色调略微发浑。</li>
    </ul>
    <p>综合效果是：感知色度在 480 nm 附近出现峰值——偏青的蓝色。</p>
    <p><strong>4. 路径长度积分与暮光颜色。</strong>眼睛观测到的辐亮度是沿视线穿过大气层的积分：太阳直射通量被 <span class="m" data-math="\exp[-\tau(\lambda)]"></span> 衰减，其中光学厚度 <span class="m" data-math="\tau(\lambda) \propto \lambda^{-4} \sec \zeta"></span>（还有气溶胶和分子吸收的贡献）；内散射光则是沿路径对源函数项求和，涉及相函数和局部光学厚度。当太阳天顶角 <span class="m" data-math="\zeta \to 90°"></span> 时，直射光束中的蓝光被消光（蓝光的 <span class="m" data-math="\tau"></span> 高，红光的 <span class="m" data-math="\tau"></span> 低），剩下偏红的直射成分和天空中的散射光——于是出现了橙色的日落。将同一套形式推广开来，还能解释为什么暮光中天空会从蓝 → 浅白 → 淡紫 → 变暗，以及为什么在非常洁净的高海拔日子（例如 4 km 以上），天顶会呈现出更深、近乎靛蓝的蓝色——内散射混合的路径更短，直射光束中短波长的消光也更少。</p>
    <p><strong>5. 模型何时失效。</strong>瑞利散射要求粒子尺寸参数 <span class="m" data-math="x = 2\pi r/\lambda \ll 1"></span>（对 N<sub>2</sub>、O<sub>2</sub>：<span class="m" data-math="r \sim 0.15"></span> nm，500 nm 处 <span class="m" data-math="x \sim 10^{-3}"></span>——完全在该适用范围内）。一旦气溶胶、水滴或冰晶的尺寸逼近 <span class="m" data-math="x \sim 1"></span>，米氏散射就接过了主导：角分布变得强烈前向尖峰化，波长依赖趋于平坦，逼近 <span class="m" data-math="1/\lambda^0"></span>–<span class="m" data-math="1/\lambda^{0.1}"></span>，于是有了白云、前向散射的晕和虹彩。因此，蓝色天空严格来说是<em>分子</em>（而非颗粒物）空气的属性——这也是为什么在有雾霾的日子里，蓝色会变得暗淡，靠近地平线的天空发白。</p>
    <p><strong>关于 <span class="m" data-math="\lambda^{-4}"></span> 中的“4”：</strong>它有时被过度神化。它其实就是两个标度关系的乘积：感应偶极矩按 <span class="m" data-math="\alpha E"></span> 缩放（小分子的极化率没有频率依赖），而偶极子的辐射功率按 <span class="m" data-math="\omega^4"></span> 缩放（这是一个远场、偶极场运动学的结果）。所以整个可见光谱的行为都由一个数——分子极化率——和基本电磁学决定。</p>
